+# Existing admission spike
+
+This is an isolated, uncompiled source spike, not an installed SDK or safe opener.
+`admit_existing` consumes an already opened Datastore and returns that same owner
+after a single read-only transaction. Its inputs are fixed identifiers, a bound
+record identity, expected public owner and explicitly required field definitions.
+No arbitrary admission SQL or caller callback is accepted. The fixed SELECT has
+limit2; namespace/database/table/field metadata lookups use existing catalog reads.
+Capabilities are temporarily none, so computed schema cannot authorize network or
+scripting. Required fields reject computed definitions. Owner/identity columns must
+have explicit String kind requirements. Missing version/catalog/owner or a mismatch
+refuses. All transaction reads, including the fixed owner SELECT, share one snapshot.
+The admission transaction is cancelled, never committed; normal capabilities are
+restored only for the consumed object's success return or teardown.
+
+This method does not open or pin paths and cannot prove that its caller used a
+strict-existing physical opener. No connector is wired to it in this spike. Its
+public placement is a reviewable API proposal, not a capability proof: final wiring
+must require the actual guarded existing-open result and call admission before
+check_version/bootstrap, connection-ready and router maintenance tasks. Ordinary
+Datastore::new is not a valid retained recipe. No close/reopen is allowed at promotion.
+
+Kind/flexible/readonly/computed checks are an explicit required-field compatibility
+contract, not proof of every schema/index/event/permission invariant. Required field
+coverage must be independently reconciled with application consumers. The admission
+five-second timeout covers the metadata/owner read body; transaction creation and
+terminal cancellation/drop still need the host's bounded startup/lifetime handling.
+SDK format validation is logical; it does not promise no physical lock/WAL writes.
+
+The separate lock and manifest helpers are not connected to CoreInner/TreeBuilder.
+They need retained Windows ancestor/catalog/lock handle identity binding, existing
+directory/optional-store policy and recovery corruption discipline before integration.
+Existing-only lock acquisition may write its PID only after it obtains exclusivity;
+normal acquisition retains its previous behavior. Existing-manifest loading bypasses
+the create branch but still uses a path: this alone is not a race/confinement proof.
+
+No application/schema/identity/launcher wiring or retained-store release is present.
+Parent review, implementation completion, compilation and disposable acceptance are
+required. See the spike report and acceptance matrix outside the copied SDK tree.
